<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slovenes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Slovenes are a South Slavic people living mainly in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> they lived in the Austrian crown lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Accounts of the Austro-Hungarian Empire list them among the nationalities of the multinational empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slovenes.jpg" alt="Slovenes" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jurij Šubic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurij_%C5%A0ubic_-_Volkstrachten_aus_Krain-Slowenen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, Hungarians, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Romanians, Croats, Serbs, <b>Slovenes</b> and Italians, with large Jewish communities in Galicia, Bohemia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It satisfied Hungarian aspirations while leaving the empire's other nationalities, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, <b>Slovenes</b>, Croats, Serbs, Romanians, Italians and others, without comparable autonomy, a permanent source of political tension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1918 the Prince Regent Alexander proclaimed the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b> in Belgrade, with his father Peter I as king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carinthian <b>Slovenes</b> and the Croats and Hungarians of Burgenland received minority rights under the State Treaty of 1955, and the Roma and Sinti were recognised in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy and the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b> signed the Treaty of Rapallo, settling their post-war border.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Podgorica Assembly deposed King Nicholas I and the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty of Montenegro and voted for unconditional union with Serbia, days before the creation of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
